Floating-Point Comparison Issue
cvoid main() {
    float f;
    f = 0.6;
    if(f == 0.6)
        printf("hello");
    else
        printf("Hai");
    // O/P: Hai   (because 0.6 is double, f is float → precision mismatch)
}

void main() {
    float f;
    f = 0.125;
    if(f == 0.125)
        printf("hello");
    else
        printf("Hai");
    // O/P: hello  (0.125 has exact binary representation)
}
Why? 0.6 cannot be represented exactly in binary (infinite repeating), but 0.125 = 2⁻³ = 1/8 → exact.
Decimal to Binary (Fractional Part)
Convert 0.125 to binary:
0.125 × 2 = 0.25  → 0
0.25  × 2 = 0.5   → 0
0.5   × 2 = 1.0   → 1
0.0   × 2 = 0.0   → stop

0.125 = .001 in binary
     = 0×2⁻¹ + 0×2⁻² + 1×2⁻³
     = 1 × 2⁻³ = 1/8 = 0.125 ✓

IEEE 754 Floating Point
float f = 3.18;
f is stored/printed using IEEE 754 Standard Floating Point Representation (binary internal format).
